<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helena of Bulgaria, Empress of Serbia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1315–<a href="/years/1374">1374</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Empress Jelena</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helena of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, called Jelena in Serbian, was the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> and empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. A sister of the Bulgarian tsar Ivan Alexander, she married Dušan in 1332 and took part in government. After his death in 1355 she ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a> and its region for some years with the help of the despot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovan-uglesa">Jovan Uglješa</a>, before becoming a nun under the name Elisaveta.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Helena descended from three prestigious medieval Balkan ruling families, the Komnenos, the Asenides and the Shishmanides. She was the daughter of the despot Sratsimir and Keratsa Petritsa, a granddaughter of Shishman I, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a>, and a niece of Tsar Michael III Shishman Asen. Besides Tsar Ivan Alexander, her brothers included the despot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-komnenos-asen">John Komnenos Asen</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanine">Kanina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Valona</a>. Through her grandmother she descended from Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> and Irene Komnina. The frescoes of the Church of the Holy Virgin in Matejce show her and her son not as Nemanjić rulers but as descendants of the Asen and Komnenos houses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> at Svrčin on 8 September 1331, and the marriage, most likely held on 26 April 1332, was arranged as part of a peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and Serbia. Their son was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a>. A daughter, Theodora, became a consort of the Ottoman Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a> at the age of twelve, and the historian John V. A. Fine identifies a possible second daughter with Irene, wife of Gregorios Preljub, the Serbian governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>.</p><p>On Dušan's death in 1355 Helena was regent for her son for two years and received lands between the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Vardar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesta">Mesta</a> and the Chalcidic peninsula, holding court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a>. Her principality there was neutral and autonomous: a note on a charter of 1360 to 1361 says that the holy tsaritsa rules alone in Serres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Helena became a nun in 1359 under the name Jelisaveta, yet remained active in politics for some time. In her last years she oversaw the rebuilding and renovation of several monastic communities. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar Monastery</a> gives the day of her death as 7 November 1374. She was canonised under the name Saint Elizabeth and her relics are venerated. According to the Bulgarian historian Plamen Pavlov, one of her hands is kept in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> box at the Savina monastery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herceg-novi">Herceg Novi</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-kotor">Bay of Kotor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>; the relics came there in 1759 and before that may have been at the Tvrdoš Monastery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebinje">Trebinje</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helena of Bulgaria, Empress of Serbia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Stefan Dušan's death in 1355, his widow, <b>Empress Jelena</b>, ruled Serres, the city he had taken from Byzantium in 1345.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-serres">Despotate of Serres</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Second Bulgarian Empire. Died in the Serbian Empire. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
